topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_6", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_7", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_8", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "engenn11le11ob06re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "The Past Perfect Tense to Show Regret "}, {"type": "describer", "content": " The Past Perfect doesn't only refer to an action prior to a past event. You can also use it to express regret. When you want to talk about something that you did or did not do and that has a negative effect on the present, use the Past Perfect."}, {"type": "text", "content": "The form of the Past Perfect is:"}, {"type": "text", "content": "subject + <span class=\"italic\">had</span> + past participle"}, {"type": "text", "content": "You can use the structure <span class=\"italic\">If only/I wish</span> + Past Perfect to express regret."}, {"type": "text", "content": "For example:<br/><span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"underline\">If only</span> <span class=\"strong\">I had talked</span> to him then, I would know how he feels. <br/><span class=\"underline\">I wish</span> <span class=\"strong\">I had woken</span> up earlier - I wouldn't have been late for work.</span>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "If only sharing had been something that the brothers had been willing to do."}, {"type": "example", "content": "If only Edward hadn't wanted to sell the house and split the money. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "If only Robert hadn't wanted, equally as much, to keep it."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Both families wished that the two brothers, who had been inseparable as children, would speak to each other."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Both wished they had known beforehand to close it up properly. "}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": " Perfect Infinitives in Third Conditional"}, {"type": "describer", "content": " You use the third conditional to describe an unreal situation with reference to the past. You imagine a hypothetical situation, something that did not happen. You use the Perfect infinitive in the result clause of the third conditional."}, {"type": "text", "content": "The structure of the third conditional is:"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"italic\">If</span> + past perfect + perfect infinitive"}, {"type": "text", "content": "For example:<br/><span class=\"italic\">If the driver <span class=\"strong\">had thought to have fastened</span> his seatbelt, he probably would have survived the accident. <br/>If we <span class=\"strong\">had decided to have booked</span> our flight earlier, it would have been cheaper.</span>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Robert said that if their father had wanted the house to have been sold he would have said so. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Edward said that their father had known the house to have been outdated and ready to sell. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Edward felt that if Robert's clan had been prepared to have taken it on and buy the other one out, that would be a possibility."}, {"type": "example", "content": "If either had wanted to have compromised, then each would said they had more right to the house than the other. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "If the news had reported to have seen widespread damage then the old place would have been in danger. "}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "will"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "\u200bofficial \u200bstatement of what should be done with \u200bmoney and \u200bproperty after a death"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: As they sat going over their father's will in the lawyer's office, the two brothers became the worst of enemies."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le11ob06aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use to talk about family disagreements."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to split"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to divide into two or more parts"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: If only Edward hadn't wanted to sell the house and split the money."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le11ob06aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "clan"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "family"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Edward felt that if Robert's clan were prepared to have taken it on and buy the other one out, that would be a possibility."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le11ob06aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to compromise"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to come to an agreement in which each \u200bside has given up something it had \u200bwanted"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: If n